---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp/img/lab-1-7-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);
}`;
const Figure2 = `(0, 0)                    (400, 0)
   +---------------------+
   |                     |
   |                     |
   |      the canvas     |
   |                     |
   |                     |
   +---------------------+
(0, 400)                (400, 400)`;
const Figure3 = `rect(50, 50, 120, 80);`;
const Figure4 = `ellipse(200, 200, 120, 80);`;
const Figure5 = `fill(255, 0, 0);
rect(20, 20, 100, 100);

fill(0, 0, 255);
rect(70, 70, 100, 100);`;
const Task1 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);
}
`;
const Task2 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);

  rect(40, 40, 120, 80);
  ellipse(280, 80, 120, 80);
  line(0, 200, 400, 200);
}
`;
const Task3 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);

  rect(40, 40, 120, 80);
  ellipse(280, 80, 120, 80);
  line(0, 200, 400, 200);

  // Below the line, draw a simple house:
  //   a square for the walls
  //   a triangle-ish roof made from two lines
  //   a small rectangle for the door
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.7: p5 Lab 1 &mdash; The Canvas</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />


        <!-- What You're Building -->
        <section id="target">
          <h2>What You're Building</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image src={TargetImage} alt="A 400 by 400 light gray canvas with an unfilled rectangle in the upper left, an unfilled ellipse in the upper right, and a horizontal line running all the way across the middle" width={400} class="img-fluid rounded" />
          </div>

          <p>By the end of <b>Task 2</b> you will have a canvas with three shapes on it, each landing exactly where its numbers put it &mdash; and a line splitting the canvas in half, ready for Task 3.</p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Task 3 is not shown.</b> That one is yours to work out.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />
        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.7</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">p5 Lab 1 &mdash; The Canvas</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>What a Lab Is</p>
              <p>The Coordinate System</p>
              <p>Drawing Shapes</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>What Is a Lab?</h2>
              <p>
                Every few activities, we step away from text output and draw
                something instead.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>What Is a Lab?</h2>
              <p>
                Labs never introduce new logic. They take something you already
                learned and make it <i>visible</i>.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Today there is no new logic at all &mdash; just a new place to
                put it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Starting a Sketch</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Starting a Sketch</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet
                      code="setup()"
                      lang={"javascript"}
                      theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                      inline
                    /> runs once, at the start
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet
                      code="createCanvas(400, 400)"
                      lang={"javascript"}
                      theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                      inline
                    /> makes a drawing area 400 wide and 400 tall
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet
                      code="background(220)"
                      lang={"javascript"}
                      theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                      inline
                    /> fills it with light gray
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Where Is (0, 0)?</h2>
              <p>Not where you expect.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Where Is (0, 0)?</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="none"
                lang="txt"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Part That Trips Everyone</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet
                  code="(0, 0)"
                  lang={"txt"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> is the <b class="text-sn-theme">top left</b>, and{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">y gets bigger going down</b>.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                In math class, y goes up. Here it goes down. Every graphics
                system on every computer works this way, and it catches people
                every single time.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Rectangles</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                x, y, then width, height. The x and y are the{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">top left corner</b>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Ellipses</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Same four numbers &mdash; but here x and y are the{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">center</b>, not the corner.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Corner vs. Center</h2>
              <p>
                A rectangle is positioned by its corner. An ellipse is positioned
                by its middle.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                This is not a rule with a good reason. It is just how it is, and
                it is the single most common cause of "why is my shape in the
                wrong place?"
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Color and Order</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure5}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Color and Order</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <CodeSnippet
                      code="fill(r, g, b)"
                      lang={"javascript"}
                      theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                      inline
                    /> sets the color of everything drawn <i>after</i> it
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    Each of r, g, b runs from 0 to 255
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    Shapes drawn later sit <b>on top of</b> shapes drawn earlier
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <p class="fragment">
                A sketch is a stack, built bottom to top, in the order you wrote
                it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Creating a canvas and setting a background</li>
                <li>Locating a point using canvas coordinates</li>
                <li>Drawing rectangles, ellipses, and lines</li>
                <li>Setting colors and controlling drawing order</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Canvas</dt>
                  <dd>The rectangular area a sketch draws into.</dd>
                  <dt>Coordinate</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A pair of numbers, x and y, giving a position on the canvas.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Origin</dt>
                  <dd>
                    The point <CodeSnippet
                      code="(0, 0)"
                      lang={"txt"}
                      theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                      inline
                    />, at the top left corner of the canvas.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Pixel</dt>
                  <dd>
                    One dot on the screen. Canvas measurements are counted in
                    these.
                  </dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js</b> sketch named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-7-canvas</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything today goes inside <CodeSnippet
              code="setup()"
              lang={"javascript"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, between its curly braces. Nothing moves yet, so nothing needs to
            repeat.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: An Empty Canvas</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              You should see a light gray square. That is the whole result, and
              it is correct.
            </li>
            <li>
              Change 400 to 600 and run again. Change <CodeSnippet
                code="background(220)"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> to <CodeSnippet
                code="background(0)"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> and run again. Then set both back.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 1 — The Canvas"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Three Shapes</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a rectangle, an ellipse, and a line.</li>
            <li>
              Before running, predict where each one will land. Sketch the canvas
              on paper if that helps.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then run it and compare. The rectangle and the ellipse use their
              four numbers differently &mdash; make sure you can see that in the
              result.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 1 — The Canvas"
            ins={{ range: "6-8" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Draw a House</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The comments at the bottom are a plan. Write the drawing code under
              them.
            </li>
            <li>
              Everything must sit <i>below</i> the line at y = 200, so every y
              value you use should be greater than 200.
            </li>
            <li>
              Give the walls and the door different colors using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="fill()"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">The roof will stay black, and that is
              correct.</b>{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="fill()"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> colors the <i>inside</i> of a shape, and a line has no inside.
              Try it anyway so you can see it do nothing.
            </li>
            <li>
              Watch your drawing order. If the door disappears, you probably drew
              the walls after it.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 1 — The Canvas"
            ins={{ range: "10-13" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): Put Something in the Exact Center</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Draw a circle whose center is the exact center of a 400 by 400
              canvas.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then draw a square whose center is also the exact center, without
              guessing and nudging.
            </li>
            <li>
              The square is genuinely harder, and working out why is the point of
              the challenge.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your finished sketch should show all of the following.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A canvas 400 by 400 with a light gray background.</li>
            <li>
              A rectangle in the upper left, and an ellipse in the upper right.
            </li>
            <li>A line running all the way across the middle.</li>
            <li>
              Below that line, a recognizable house with walls, a roof, and a
              door &mdash; the walls and the door in different colors.
            </li>
            <li>The door visible, not buried under the walls.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            You should also be able to point at any shape and say which numbers
            in your code put it there.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              On this canvas, y increases as you move <i>down</i>. Describe a
              mistake you made, or nearly made, because of that.
            </li>
            <li>
              A rectangle is placed by its corner and an ellipse by its center.
              Explain how you would place a rectangle so that its <i>center</i>{" "}
              lands on a specific point.
            </li>
            <li>
              Drawing order decides what covers what. How is that similar to the
              order statements run in the text programs you wrote in Activities
              1.1 through 1.6?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
